 Every view of the clock is reachable with the pointer alone.

 The pane had three views and one editing mode, and the only road between
 them was a scancode: `z`, `f` and `s` off the face, Esc back. Nothing in
 the tree named them and the desk routed no click to the pane while the
 face was up, so a reader who did not know the keys had one screen.

 THE ROW THAT FAILS WHEN THE STRIP IS GONE IS THE POINT. Each row below
 finds the tab's own rectangle by scanning the laid tree for its id, and a
 tab that is not in the tree yields no point: the scan answers -1, the row
 prints `no`, and removing `clk-nav` from any view moves it. A row that
 only asked what the view became after a call at a fixed coordinate would
 pass on an empty strip, because a miss leaves the view alone and the face
 is what the block already held.

Section: Test

  hit-id : WidgetNode, Integer, Integer -> Text
  hit-id (laid) (x) (y) =
    when ev-hit-widget laid x y
      is Just (wgt) -> wgt.wn-id
      is None -> ""

  fp-row : WidgetNode, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  fp-row (laid) (id) (x) (y) =
    if x >= 1024 then 0 - 1
    else if hit-id laid x y == id then x
    else fp-row laid id (x + 8) y

  fp : WidgetNode, Text, Integer -> Integer
  fp (laid) (id) (y) =
    if y >= 768 then 0 - 1
    else let x = fp-row laid id 0 y
    in if x >= 0 then x * 1000 + y else fp laid id (y + 8)

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "no"

 One tab pressed from whatever view the block currently holds. Answers the
 click function's own answer, and -1 when the tab had no rectangle to press.

 THE SCAN FINDS A LOGICAL POINT AND THE CLICK TAKES A DEVICE ONE. `ev-hit-widget`
 walks the laid tree, which `desk-clock-laid` builds in logical units, while
 `desk-widget-hit` is handed what the mouse reports and divides by the scale
 itself. The two coincide at 1024, where the scale is 1, so a test written
 without the multiply passes everywhere it is cheap to look and fails only
 above 1600. That is what the 1920 row below is for, and it caught this
 instrument before it graded anything.

  press : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Integer
  press (ds) (ks) (w) (id) =
    let laid = desk-clock-laid w 768 gfont-none ds ks
    in let p = fp laid id 0
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in if p < 0 then 0 - 1
    else desk-clock-nav-click laid ((p / 1000) * s) ((int-mod p 1000) * s) s ks

 A point that is on no tab at all, taken from the pane's own art box, which
 every view reserves and no tab overlaps.

  off-nav : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  off-nav (ds) (ks) (w) =
    let laid = desk-clock-laid w 768 gfont-none ds ks
    in let b = desk-clock-box laid "clk-art" (ui-wscale w)
    in desk-clock-nav-click laid (b.lr-x + b.lr-w / 2) (b.lr-y + b.lr-h / 2) (ui-wscale w) ks

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed dk-ds-bytes 64
    in let ks = alloc-zeroed 128 64
    in let ap = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell dk-adorn-default
    in let gp = poke-32 ds desk-gpu-cell 1
    in let cp = poke-32 ds desk-clock-cell ks
    in let a1 = press ds ks 1024 "clk-nav-tab-1"
    in let v1 = clk-view ks
    in let a2 = press ds ks 1024 "clk-nav-tab-2"
    in let v2 = clk-view ks
    in let a0 = press ds ks 1024 "clk-nav-tab-0"
    in let v0 = clk-view ks
    in let a3 = press ds ks 1024 "clk-nav-tab-3"
    in let v3 = clk-view ks
    in let r0 = poke-32 ks clk-editing-cell 0
    in let r1 = clk-set-view ks clk-view-face
    in let miss = off-nav ds ks 1024
    in let hd = press ds ks 1920 "clk-nav-tab-1"
    in let hv = clk-view ks
    in act
      print-line-uni ("world tab: " & yn (a1 == 1 & v1 == clk-view-zones))
      print-line-uni ("formats tab: " & yn (a2 == 1 & v2 == clk-view-formats))
      print-line-uni ("clock tab: " & yn (a0 == 1 & v0 == clk-view-face))
      print-line-uni ("set tab: " & yn (a3 == 3 & v3 == clk-view-face))
      print-line-uni ("off the strip: " & yn (miss == desk-clock-nav-none))
      print-line-uni ("at 1920: " & yn (hd == 1 & hv == clk-view-zones))
    end
